   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the alcoholic beverage control law, in relation to allowing off-premise licensees to conduct educational seminars",
    "basePrintNo": "S5844",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S5844-2017",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 42,
       "fullName": "John J. Bonacic",
       "imgName": "413_john_j._bonacic.jpg",
       "incumbent": false,
       "memberId": 413,
       "sessionMemberId": 1016,
       "sessionYear": 2017,
       "shortName": "BONACIC"
      }
     ],
     "size": 1
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §63, ABC L",
    "lawSection": "Alcoholic Beverage Control Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S5844\n\n TITLE OF BILL :  An act to amend the alcoholic beverage control law,\nin relation to allowing off-premise licensees to conduct educational\nseminars\n\n PURPOSE :\n\nThis legislation would allow retailers to charge a fee for providing\neducational seminars to their customers without being considered\nconducting another business.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection one and two amend subdivision 4 of § 63 of the Alcoholic\nBeverage Control Law to allow off-premise licensees to charge a fee\nfor educational seminars in their stores.\n\nSection three establishes the effective date.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nEducational seminars enhance customers' knowledge and appreciation of\nwines and spirits, including New York products. With so many choices\nof products, seminars help customers become better educated; for\nexample, on varieties of wines, distilling processes of spirits, and\nthe difference between liqueurs and cordials. With this knowledge,\ncustomers can make better choices and are more likely to try new\nproducts.\n\nAllowing retailers to charge a fee covers the cost of the product used\nin the seminars and allows retailers to pay experts to come to their\nstores to conduct seminars. It also gives credibility to the\nretailers' expertise in making recommendations. Retailers can impart\ntheir knowledge to their customers and in doing so, increase sales of\nalcoholic beverages, encourage responsible drinking, and allow for\nthis additional revenue stream for the licensee.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nNew bill.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nAdditional sales and excise taxes to the State due to increased sales\nof alcoholic beverages.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
